 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Committed revisions are pushed to connected staff browsers via server-managed WebSockets rather than periodic polling or a hosted realtime service. Polling was rejected due to repeated read load and latency lag; a hosted realtime service was rejected to avoid recurring vendor fees and provider lock-in. Self-hosted WebSockets trade off taking on server connection capacity and reconnection/replay management to deliver fast, low-latency updates independently. (See decision doc: `docs/decisions/change-delivery-approach.md`).
